\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{The residual action splits an equivariant endomorphism}
\date{2026-11-21}

\p{Let #{h} be the order-#{3} stabilizer element of
\ref{fdip-0126}, acting on #{\mathbb C^2} by the faithful
homomorphism of that example, and let #{v} be the vector it
fixes. In a basis of eigenvectors the action is diagonal, with
eigenvalues #{1} and a root #{\mu} of #{t^2+t+1}. An
endomorphism #{Y(v)} that commutes with this action is
diagonal in the same basis. The eigenvalues of
#{m{\bbI}+Y(v)} are #{m+y_1} and #{m+y_2}.}

\proof{
\p{\ref{fdip-0126} sends the class #{[u,m]} to the matrix
#{\gamma^m M(u)}. That map is a homomorphism into #{U(2)}, and
it is faithful. The same example sets #{v=(1,\alpha)}, shows
that #{h} fixes #{v}, shows that #{h^3} is the identity, and
shows that #{h} is distinct from the identity. Faithfulness
therefore sends #{h} to a matrix other than the identity.
The homomorphism sends #{h^3} to the cube of that matrix, so
the cube is the identity matrix. The first coordinate of #{v}
is #{1}, so #{v} is nonzero and extends to a basis #{v,w} of
#{\mathbb C^2}. The first column of the matrix is #{(1,0)}.
Write the image of #{w} as #{av+\mu w}. The cube of the matrix
sends #{w} to #{a(1+\mu+\mu^2)v+\mu^3 w}. Independence of the
basis equates coefficients with those of #{w}, so
#{\mu^3=1} and #{a(1+\mu+\mu^2)=0}. If #{\mu=1}, then
#{3a=0}. In the scalar field of \ref{fdip-0126}, #{3\ne0}, so
#{a=0} and the matrix is the identity. Faithfulness excludes
that matrix, so #{\mu\ne1}. In that field
#{\mu^3-1=({\mu}-1)(\mu^2+\mu+1)=0}, and the first factor is
nonzero, so #{\mu^2+\mu+1=0}. The vector
#{w+\frac{a}{{\mu}-1}v} is nonzero and is an eigenvector for
#{\mu}.}

\p{At the fixed vector, equivariance of a
[Yukawa map](fdip-011M) says that #{Y(v)} commutes with the
matrix of #{h}. Let #{u} be the second eigenvector. A general
vector is #{xv+zu}. Applying the matrix multiplies the second
coordinate by #{\mu}. The result equals the original vector
exactly when #{z=0}, so the eigenspace for #{1} is the line
through #{v}. It equals #{\mu} times the original vector
exactly when #{x=0}, so the eigenspace for #{\mu} is the line
through #{u}. Commutation says that #{Y(v)} applied after the
matrix equals the matrix applied after #{Y(v)}. The matrix
fixes #{v}, so #{Y(v)v} is fixed and lies on the line through
#{v}. On #{u} the matrix acts by #{\mu}, so the same identity
sends #{Y(v)u} to #{\mu} times itself, and that image lies on
the line through #{u}. In this basis #{Y(v)} is diagonal.
Write its diagonal entries as #{y_1} and #{y_2}. Adding
#{m{\bbI}} adds #{m} to each of them, so the eigenvalues are
#{m+y_1} and #{m+y_2}.}
}
